✅ QUESTION 3: LONGEST BITONIC SUBSEQUENCE (LBS)

This is a classic DP + LIS question and a favorite in product-based companies.

🔹 Problem Statement

You are given an integer array nums.

A Bitonic Subsequence is a sequence that:

First strictly increases

Then strictly decreases

Your task is to find the length of the Longest Bitonic Subsequence.

📌 The increasing or decreasing part must have at least one element.

✏️ Example
Input:  nums = [1, 11, 2, 10, 4, 5, 2, 1]
Output: 6

One valid bitonic subsequence:
1 → 2 → 10 → 5 → 2 → 1

🔹 Concept Breakdown

We break the problem into two LIS problems:

LIS from Left to Right

LDS from Right to Left (LIS in reverse)

For each index i:

BitonicLength[i] = LIS[i] + LDS[i] − 1


(We subtract 1 because nums[i] is counted twice)

🔹 Algorithm (Step-by-Step)
Step 1: Compute LIS[] array

LIS[i] → Longest Increasing Subsequence ending at i

Step 2: Compute LDS[] array

LDS[i] → Longest Decreasing Subsequence starting from i

Step 3: Combine results

Find max of LIS[i] + LDS[i] − 1

⏱ Time Complexity: O(n²)
📦 Space Complexity: O(n)

🔹 Java Code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int longestBitonicSequence(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int[] lis = new int[n];
        int[] lds = new int[n];

        // Step 1: Initialize
        Arrays.fill(lis, 1);
        Arrays.fill(lds, 1);

        // Step 2: LIS from left
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                if (nums[i] > nums[j]) {
                    lis[i] = Math.max(lis[i], lis[j] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 3: LDS from right
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            for (int j = n - 1; j > i; j--) {
                if (nums[i] > nums[j]) {
                    lds[i] = Math.max(lds[i], lds[j] + 1);
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 4: Find maximum bitonic length
        int maxLen = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, lis[i] + lds[i] - 1);
        }

        return maxLen;
    }
}

🔹 Line-by-Line Explanation
int[] lis = new int[n];
int[] lds = new int[n];


Stores increasing and decreasing subsequence lengths.

Arrays.fill(lis, 1);
Arrays.fill(lds, 1);


Each element itself is a subsequence of length 1.

LIS Calculation
if (nums[i] > nums[j]) {
    lis[i] = Math.max(lis[i], lis[j] + 1);
}


If current number is greater, extend LIS.

LDS Calculation
if (nums[i] > nums[j]) {
    lds[i] = Math.max(lds[i], lds[j] + 1);
}


Same logic, but traversing from the right.

Final Result
maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, lis[i] + lds[i] - 1);


Combines both sequences.

🔹 Dry Run (Simplified)
nums = [1, 11, 2, 10, 4, 5, 2, 1]

LIS = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 2, 1]
LDS = [1, 5, 2, 4, 3, 3, 2, 1]

Bitonic = LIS + LDS - 1
Max = 6

